 "indicator": {
  "definition": {
   "definition": "Bankers' deposits are the current-account balances that banks hold with the Reserve Bank, held mainly to satisfy the Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR) and as working funds for clearing and settlement. They are one of the three components of reserve money (reserve money = currency in circulation + bankers' deposits with the RBI + 'other' deposits with the RBI) and appear as item 2.4 'Scheduled Commercial Banks' within the 'Deposits' block of the WSS 'RBI — Liabilities and Assets' table. Because they are the balance the banking system keeps at the central bank, their growth is a direct read on system liquidity and the reserves banks are putting to work.",
   "source_name": "Reserve Bank of India — Handbook on the Weekly Statistical Supplement, 'Reserve Money: Components and Sources' and 'RBI – Liabilities and Assets'",
   "source_url": "https://rbidocs.rbi.org.in/rdocs/Publications/PDFs/HWSSF170614.pdf",
